  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1038 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nThe extended forecast package remains on track this evening, with \nthe main theme being warmer temperatures and drier conditions \nthrough the work week into next weekend. The main change? There is \nsome hope for isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms by \nlate week. Synoptically speaking, the upper level ridge over the \nSWCONUS will begin to weaken through mid-week as a series of \nshortwaves translate through the PacNW regions, leading to a weak \nomega block pattern across the CONUS. Though heights will be near \nstationary, surface winds will be persistent out of the south-\nsouthwest in response to lee cyclogenesis to our north. As a result, \nincreased thickness values will aid in afternoon temperatures above \nseasonal standards through the work week, climbing into the 80s and \n90s across the Caprock regions. Though confidence is low, NBM PoPs \nare trying to suggest the potential for a rogue shower and/or \nthunderstorm Thursday afternoon in response to a disturbance \ntracking near the US/Mexico border. Though, deterministic and \nensemble models are not in great agreement with this, keeping the \nbulk of moisture and forcing well to our south. Depending on how \nthis system evolves, along with the amount of moisture return we are \nable to see will determine if areas across our southern row of \ncounties will luck out. For now, will maintain NBM non-mentionable \n(though not zero) PoPs until we getter better agreement from models. \nOtherwise, expect the drier and warming trend to continue through \nthe weekend.",
